    "narration": "I worked on a project to address a critical issue in our supervised fine-tuning (SFT) pipeline that was causing our models to produce unwanted behaviors. Specifically, we were experiencing a problem where our models would regenerate opening greetings, which is not desired in our chatbot applications.\n\nThe problem originated from our training process, where the loss was being applied to the entire flattened ChatML row. This included parts of the conversation that the model should not be regenerating, such as the initial greeting. This mismatch between training and serving was inadvertently teaching our models, including every LoRA that processed those rows, to exhibit banned behaviors like re-greeting users.\n\nTo solve this, I developed a pure-Python script called `loss_mask.py`. This script derives the assistant marker from the tokenizer chat template and applies a mask through the last assistant prefix, ensuring that only the final assistant response is scored. I also implemented a fail-safe full-row mask to prevent any unintended side effects. Additionally, I integrated this solution into our training pipeline by wiring the `FinalResponseOnlyCollator` into `train.py`. I made sure the solution was unit-testable off-pod and documented the process in both beginner and architecture notes for team ownership.\n\nBy implementing this solution, we ensured that only the final gold assistant span is scored, with the greeting and history serving as context only. This aligned our SFT process with sipagent turn ownership, preventing the model from learning and reinforcing the re-greeting behavior. As a result, we avoided wasting significant GPU resources\u2014estimated at hours to days per bad training recipe\u2014by preventing unnecessary retraining on flawed data."
