    "narration": "I worked on a project to address a significant risk in our machine learning models where client-specific greetings were being inadvertently memorized by our shared LoRA models. This was crucial because it posed a potential product risk by leaking tenant-specific configurations across different clients.\n\nThe issue arose when client-literal greetings appeared in our system notes and the opening turns of our assistant. These greetings were specific to each tenant's text-to-speech (TTS) configuration and were not meant to be generalized or transferred across clients. This leakage could lead to a breach of client-specific configurations, which was out of the training scope as defined by our client, the stakeholder.\n\nTo tackle this, I introduced a choke point in the assembler to replace the literal greetings with a placeholder `[greeting already spoken]` in both system notes and the opening assistant turn. This ensured that the real greeting was only used for dialogue invention. Additionally, I implemented a defense-in-depth strategy using response-only loss masking to further protect against this leakage. I preserved the row shape for easy spot checking and trainer use, added a suite of tests (`test_greeting_stripped_from_rows.py`), and documented these changes in our architecture and status reports.\n\nAs a result, we successfully removed literal greetings from the `messages` and `instruction` fields in our training JSONL files, leading to cleaner cross-tenant adapters. This proactive approach avoided the need for a costly and time-consuming full regeneration of the data to scrub greetings later. The estimated savings include the engineer and generation costs associated with such a scrub pass.\n\nI implemented a solution to prevent client-specific greetings from being memorized by our LoRA models, reducing product risk and saving on potential data regeneration costs."
