    "narration": "In one of my recent projects, I tackled a significant issue involving our AI's dialogue capabilities. The AI was generating responses that promised actions it couldn't fulfill, like booking appointments or checking calendars. This was crucial to address because it could lead to user frustration and mistrust in our system.\n\nThe problem arose when our AI, powered by LoRA, began generating responses that suggested it could perform CRM or booking tasks. However, these capabilities were not supported by our system, which only allowed for simple actions like transferring calls or sending messages. This disconnect between what the AI promised and what it could actually do was misleading to users.\n\nTo resolve this, I first cataloged the types of invented capabilities the AI was generating, such as lookup and booking promises. I then conducted a series of audits using our jul14d framework, focusing on five to seven key flags. This helped identify where the AI was going off track. I implemented coherence and capability guards to prevent these misleading responses from being regenerated. Additionally, I documented any frozen rows to maintain transparency and avoid rewriting history. I also communicated these issues clearly to our team lead, the stakeholder, via plain-English updates on Slack.\n\nAs a result of these efforts, we were able to reclassify major defects and block problematic response patterns before they could contaminate our training data. This proactive approach saved us from potentially having to regenerate a full golden file and retrain the model, which would have required approximately 4.5 hours of GPU time. This not only saved time but also preserved the integrity of our training data.\n\nI implemented capability guards to prevent our AI from making false promises, saving us from a costly retrain and ensuring user trust in our system's capabilities."
