    "narration": "I worked on optimizing our training data for a machine learning model by addressing discrepancies between our training and real-world data. This was crucial because our model's performance in production heavily depended on how well it learned from realistic scenarios.\n\nThe issue was that our training data had a uniform mix of open and closed conversation paths and often started with an unrealistic \"dump all slots\" approach. This resulted in the model learning from paths that were not representative of actual user interactions, especially wasting resources on rare-edge cases that didn't occur naturally.\n\nTo tackle this, I developed a golden-hop catalog that included hours-collapsed IDs and marked unsupported paths as hidden. I implemented RPG sampling to achieve a 75% mix of open and leave-message-while-open scenarios. Additionally, I created an opening-slot sampler that focused heavily on issue-only paths, aligning with our production mix where all-three slots were rare or nonexistent. I documented the hop ID map and set diversity targets to ensure the training data was representative of real-world interactions.\n\nThis approach led to a significant improvement in the model's ability to hit rare-edge cases during generated conversations. The training distribution became much closer to what we see in live scenarios. Specifically, the first-turn \"all-three\" slot usage dropped from 29.4% to our target of 0%, aligning with production-like conditions. As a result, we reduced unnecessary spending on Haiku by avoiding unrealistic full-slot dumps, saving a fraction of each generate batch."
