    "opening": "I worked on a project to improve the accuracy of our call handling system, specifically addressing the issue of incorrect after-hours messaging. This was crucial because it directly impacted how calls were routed and wrapped up, affecting both customer satisfaction and operational efficiency.",
    "situation": "The problem was that our system often mishandled calls outside of business hours. Callers would hear daytime greetings when they should have received after-hours messages. This was due to our models frequently choosing the wrong action, like `TAKE_MESSAGE` instead of `END_CALL`, because the business hours were treated as dynamic text rather than fixed global settings.",
    "approach": "To tackle this, I implemented a solution that locked the `open_now` variable, similar to how we handle company names, to ensure consistent behavior. I created an after-hours greeting template that didn't require adding a new `GREETING` state to the state machine (SM). This involved setting up rules for the hours based on different roles such as inventors, callers, and agents, and ensuring that closed greetings included the correct hours. I chose this approach to avoid the complexity and ownership issues that would come with rewriting the entire state machine and its 26-edge quotas. I discussed the pros and cons of this approach with the stakeholder on Slack, ultimately deciding on the lock-over-new-state method.",
    "outcome": "The result was a more reliable system that consistently handled open and closed hours correctly in both generation and Mode C. This approach avoided the potential churn of adding a new `GREETING` state, which would have required significant changes across the stack. By doing this, we estimated saving weeks of work that would have been needed for a full state machine rewrite, runtime adjustments, and golden edge-list updates. Additionally, it prevented unnecessary after-hours live transfer attempts, streamlining our operations.",
    "oneLiner": "",
    "narration": "I worked on a project to improve the accuracy of our call handling system, specifically addressing the issue of incorrect after-hours messaging. This was crucial because it directly impacted how calls were routed and wrapped up, affecting both customer satisfaction and operational efficiency.\n\nThe problem was that our system often mishandled calls outside of business hours. Callers would hear daytime greetings when they should have received after-hours messages. This was due to our models frequently choosing the wrong action, like `TAKE_MESSAGE` instead of `END_CALL`, because the business hours were treated as dynamic text rather than fixed global settings.\n\nTo tackle this, I implemented a solution that locked the `open_now` variable, similar to how we handle company names, to ensure consistent behavior. I created an after-hours greeting template that didn't require adding a new `GREETING` state to the state machine (SM). This involved setting up rules for the hours based on different roles such as inventors, callers, and agents, and ensuring that closed greetings included the correct hours. I chose this approach to avoid the complexity and ownership issues that would come with rewriting the entire state machine and its 26-edge quotas. I discussed the pros and cons of this approach with the stakeholder on Slack, ultimately deciding on the lock-over-new-state method.\n\nThe result was a more reliable system that consistently handled open and closed hours correctly in both generation and Mode C. This approach avoided the potential churn of adding a new `GREETING` state, which would have required significant changes across the stack. By doing this, we estimated saving weeks of work that would have been needed for a full state machine rewrite, runtime adjustments, and golden edge-list updates. Additionally, it prevented unnecessary after-hours live transfer attempts, streamlining our operations."
