    "approach": "To address this, I ensured that the evaluation set, named gold-set-aug-4 with 200 rows, was kept strictly for evaluation purposes only. I trained the models on a separate set, golden-1060, which I expanded to around 4385 agent-turn rows. This approach allowed for an apples-to-apples comparison by using the same 200-row evaluation set across models. Additionally, I documented the rationale in a train-vs-eval explainer for the stakeholder and resisted any pressure to use the evaluation data for training.",
    "outcome": "By maintaining a strict separation between training and evaluation data, we achieved a fair comparison between our model (#12) and the receptionist model, as well as other bake-off models, using a shared holdout set. This clarity was especially important when our live baseline lacked a valid holdout. We estimated that this approach saved us from the costs associated with generating a new holdout set and the potential loss of trust from publishing inflated scores due to data leakage.",
    "oneLiner": "I ensured the integrity of our model evaluation by maintaining a strict holdout set, preventing data leakage, and providing a fair comparison framework, saving us from costly re-evaluation and trust issues.",
    "narration": "In one of my recent projects, I tackled a critical issue in our model evaluation process for the early receptionist LoRA. The integrity of our evaluation metrics was at stake, and ensuring a fair comparison was essential for reliable model deployment.\n\nThe challenge arose when we realized that our training process included all available data without any holdout set, which led to an unfair comparison between the training set size of 1060 rows and an evaluation set of only 200 rows. This setup was tempting us to train on the evaluation gold set, which would have resulted in overly optimistic Mode C scores, making them unreliable for publication.\n\nTo address this, I ensured that the evaluation set, named gold-set-aug-4 with 200 rows, was kept strictly for evaluation purposes only. I trained the models on a separate set, golden-1060, which I expanded to around 4385 agent-turn rows. This approach allowed for an apples-to-apples comparison by using the same 200-row evaluation set across models. Additionally, I documented the rationale in a train-vs-eval explainer for the stakeholder and resisted any pressure to use the evaluation data for training.\n\nBy maintaining a strict separation between training and evaluation data, we achieved a fair comparison between our model (#12) and the receptionist model, as well as other bake-off models, using a shared holdout set. This clarity was especially important when our live baseline lacked a valid holdout. We estimated that this approach saved us from the costs associated with generating a new holdout set and the potential loss of trust from publishing inflated scores due to data leakage.\n\nI ensured the integrity of our model evaluation by maintaining a strict holdout set, preventing data leakage, and providing a fair comparison framework, saving us from costly re-evaluation and trust issues."
