    "narration": "I worked on a project where I addressed a critical issue with our system's output format that affected the usability of our live models. The project was essential because it ensured our system's compatibility and prevented unnecessary engineering debt.\n\nOur system, after scoring, often emitted outputs in the `[next_state]` or bare words format, which our sipagent could not parse because it only accepted angle-bracketed XML. This discrepancy rendered our live models unusable despite their prior high performance scores. The format inconsistency was causing state machine transitions to fail and introduced incorrect text-to-speech outputs.\n\nI tackled this problem by first distinguishing between the Mode C path and the live path to address dtype and mount ambiguities. I audited the training data, ensuring it was already in the correct angle format. I then designed a smoke test to achieve a tag-shape accuracy of at least 95% before proceeding with a full Mode C deployment. I also developed an angle-discipline package and collaborated with the stakeholder to explore options, ultimately deciding against modifying sipagent to accept square brackets as a permanent solution.\n\nThe project successfully demonstrated that on August 13, Mode C was not square-dominant, achieving a 99.4% compliance with the angle regex format. This reframed the issue as one of format and decode measurement rather than a fundamental system flaw. By avoiding a permanent sipagent format fork, we saved an estimated 4.5 hours of training time and reduced potential engineering debt and GPU usage."
