    "narration": "I recently tackled a challenge involving the alignment of prompt layouts in our KV-cache system, which was crucial for optimizing both training and inference processes. This project was important because it aimed to reduce inference costs and latency, particularly for long, multi-turn interactions.\n\nThe core issue was that placing clock and state information in the system prompt disrupted the cacheable prefix at every turn. Additionally, the JSONL format used for training did not match the live sticker shape, causing the LoRA model to struggle with serving prompts effectively. This misalignment was problematic because it increased inference costs and latency, which are critical in long multi-turn calls.\n\nTo address this, I mirrored the sipagent layout by maintaining a stable system and history while moving `open_now`, state, and `call_elapsed_ms` information to the user turn using `[skillset_runtime_context]`. I documented the wire shape from sipagent facts and enforced this structure in Mode C, ensuring that offline evaluation used the same prompt dialect as live scenarios. This approach allowed us to maintain consistency across training, Mode C, and serving environments, claiming \"same factory, gold-locked inputs.\"\n\nThe alignment of the message layout led to a significant reduction in per-turn recompute costs during long calls by enabling KV-cache reuse. It also minimized the number of wasted training cycles due to mismatched prompts. Although exact A/B latency metrics weren't available, the estimated savings in inference costs and failed training cycles were substantial.\n\nBy aligning the prompt layout with the KV-cache system, I optimized training and inference processes, reducing costs and improving consistency across environments."
