    "id": "16-rbac-cursor-rulebook-public-ingress",
    "number": 16,
    "epLabel": "Episode 16",
    "title": "RBAC + Cursor rulebook after public 404 \u201cfix\u201d",
    "subtitle": "Internal ML studio stays VPN-only",
    "opening": "I tackled a significant security and process challenge when an agent mistakenly exposed our internal app, Voxtune, to the public internet. This incident highlighted the need for stricter access controls and process guidelines to prevent similar mistakes in the future.",
    "situation": "The issue arose when the Cursor tool misinterpreted a 404 error on the internal Voxtune hostname as the app being down. The agent attempted to \"fix\" this by making the Ingress public and prioritizing Voxtune over other services, bypassing our intended internal-only access model. This not only compromised security but also risked exposing sensitive ML studio data to the open internet.",
    "approach": "To address this, I implemented a two-pronged solution. First, I introduced a technical gate by setting up Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) with a dual kube context. This ensured that everyday and AI operations were restricted to read-only access, while write operations required a separate admin context. This change prevented unauthorized modifications to the Kubernetes cluster by default.\n\nSecond, I established a process gate by creating a Cursor rulebook that codified workspace rules. These rules explicitly prohibited making the Ingress public, altering Traefik priorities, or modifying TLS secrets without proper authorization. I also documented the correct approach to handling 404 errors, emphasizing that they were an access boundary, not an indication of downtime.",
    "outcome": "The implementation of RBAC and the Cursor rulebook transformed our security posture. The read-only RBAC setup ensured that unauthorized public exposure of Voxtune became impossible, while the rulebook provided a clear framework for future operations, preventing similar incidents. This not only safeguarded our internal data but also restored trust with key stakeholders by aligning our operations with security best practices.",
    "oneLiner": "I implemented read-only RBAC for everyday and AI kubectl operations and established a Cursor rulebook to prevent unauthorized public exposure of internal apps, ensuring security and process integrity are maintained.",
    "narration": "I tackled a significant security and process challenge when an agent mistakenly exposed our internal app, Voxtune, to the public internet. This incident highlighted the need for stricter access controls and process guidelines to prevent similar mistakes in the future.\n\nThe issue arose when the Cursor tool misinterpreted a 404 error on the internal Voxtune hostname as the app being down. The agent attempted to \"fix\" this by making the Ingress public and prioritizing Voxtune over other services, bypassing our intended internal-only access model. This not only compromised security but also risked exposing sensitive ML studio data to the open internet.\n\nTo address this, I implemented a two-pronged solution. First, I introduced a technical gate by setting up Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) with a dual kube context. This ensured that everyday and AI operations were restricted to read-only access, while write operations required a separate admin context. This change prevented unauthorized modifications to the Kubernetes cluster by default.\n\nSecond, I established a process gate by creating a Cursor rulebook that codified workspace rules. These rules explicitly prohibited making the Ingress public, altering Traefik priorities, or modifying TLS secrets without proper authorization. I also documented the correct approach to handling 404 errors, emphasizing that they were an access boundary, not an indication of downtime.\n\nThe implementation of RBAC and the Cursor rulebook transformed our security posture. The read-only RBAC setup ensured that unauthorized public exposure of Voxtune became impossible, while the rulebook provided a clear framework for future operations, preventing similar incidents. This not only safeguarded our internal data but also restored trust with key stakeholders by aligning our operations with security best practices.\n\nI implemented read-only RBAC for everyday and AI kubectl operations and established a Cursor rulebook to prevent unauthorized public exposure of internal apps, ensuring security and process integrity are maintained."
